Step 13: Validate the complete Kustomize configuration

Purpose: Make sure Kustomize can successfully combine all our YAML files before we deploy them.

Run:

kubectl kustomize .


Step 1: List all your Minikube profiles

bash
minikube profile list
Step 2: If you want to switch to it explicitly

Once you confirm the name (likely quiz-app), set it as active:

bash
minikube profile quiz-app

Step 3: Confirm it's running (or start it)

bash
minikube status -p quiz-app

If it shows Running for everything, we're good to go. If it shows stopped/not running, start it with:

bash
minikube start -p quiz-app
 
 Step 2: Load both images into the Minikube cluster
 minikube image load quiz-app_backend:latest -p quiz-app
minikube image load quiz-app_frontend:latest -p quiz-app

     Step 3: Confirm they loaded
bash
minikube image ls -p quiz-app | grep quiz-app

 Step 3: Get a reachable URL for the frontend via Minikube

Since both services are NodePort, Minikube gives you an easy way to open it:

bash
minikube service frontend-service -n quiz-app-namespace -p quiz-app --url

This prints a URL like http://192.168.x.x:3xxxx.

Step 4: Test it

bash
curl -I <the URL from step 3>

Step 1: Create the two ECR repositories

bash
aws ecr create-repository --repository-name quiz-app-backend --region us-east-1
aws ecr create-repository --repository-name quiz-app-frontend --region us-east-1

Step 2: Authenticate Docker to ECR
This logs your local Docker into your private ECR registry so you're allowed to push to it:
bash
aws ecr get-login-password --region us-east-1 | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin 143353052589.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com
You should see Login Succeeded.
Step 3: Tag your existing local images with these URIs

Docker needs the image tagged with the full registry path before it can push there:
bash
docker tag quiz-app_backend:latest 143353052589.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/quiz-app-backend:latest
docker tag quiz-app_frontend:latest 143353052589.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/quiz-app-frontend:latest

Step 4: Push both images
bash
docker push 143353052589.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/quiz-app-backend:latest
docker push 143353052589.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/quiz-app-frontend:latest

This might take a few minutes depending on your upload speed — the frontend image especially, since it bundles audio files.

Step 5: Verify both landed in ECR
bash
aws ecr list-images --repository-name quiz-app-backend --region us-east-1
aws ecr list-images --repository-name quiz-app-frontend --region us-east-1

step 1: Create a terraform folder inside your project
File	                    Purpose
providers.tf	Tells Terraform "use AWS, in this region"
variables.tf	Settings you can change later without touching the logic (region, cluster name, node size/count)
vpc.tf	Builds the network: 2 public + 2 private subnets across 2 AZs, a NAT gateway so private nodes can reach the internet, and the critical AWS tags that let LoadBalancer services auto-discover subnets later
eks.tf	The actual EKS control plane + a managed node group (2 t3.medium EC2 instances by default) sitting in the private subnets from vpc.tf
outputs.tf	After creation, prints the exact command you'll need to connect kubectl to this new cluster
Step 1: Run terraform init
After apply succeeds: connect kubectl

Terraform will print an output called configure_kubectl — copy that exact command and run it. It'll look like:

bash
aws eks update-kubeconfig --region us-east-1 --name quiz-app-cluster

This switches kubectl from talking to Minikube to talking to your real EKS cluster.

Update your manifests (back in PROJECT6/, not the terraform folder)

backend-deployment.yml — change the image: line:

yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: backend-deployment
spec:
  replicas: 1

  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: backend

  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: backend

    spec:
      containers:
        - name: backend
          image: 143353052589.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/quiz-app-backend:latest
          imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent

          ports:
            - containerPort: 2323

          envFrom:
            - secretRef:
                name: backend-secret

frontend-deployment.yml — same idea:

yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: frontend-deployment
spec:
  replicas: 1

  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: frontend

  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: frontend

    spec:
      containers:
        - name: frontend
          image: 143353052589.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/quiz-app-frontend:latest
          imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent

          ports:
            - containerPort: 80

frontend-service.yml — switch to LoadBalancer so AWS provisions a public entry point:

yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: frontend-service
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer

  selector:
    app: frontend

  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 80

backend-service.yml — one small improvement: since nginx reaches the backend internally (never from the public internet directly), it doesn't need NodePort anymore — ClusterIP (the default, internal-only type) is cleaner and more secure:

yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: backend-service
spec:
  type: ClusterIP

  selector:
    app: backend

  ports:
    - port: 2323
      targetPort: 2323


