TO BRING IT UP LATER 111111

Step 1: Recreate the AWS infrastructure

bash
cd ~/Desktop/"ONLINE  DEV CLASS"/PROJECT6/terraform
terraform apply

Type yes. Takes ~15-20 minutes again (EKS control planes are always slow to create, no way around that). This rebuilds the VPC, cluster, and node group from scratch, since you destroyed them.

Step 2: Reconnect kubectl to the (newly recreated) cluster

bash
aws eks update-kubeconfig --region us-east-1 --name quiz-app-cluster

Step 3: Deploy your app

bash
cd ~/Desktop/"ONLINE  DEV CLASS"/PROJECT6
kubectl apply -k .

Step 4: Get your new LoadBalancer URL

bash
kubectl get svc -n quiz-app-namespace

Note: this will be a different hostname than before — AWS doesn't reuse the old one.



Step 1: DELETE the Kubernetes resources first (this tells AWS to tear down the load balancer)

bash
cd ~/Desktop/"ONLINE  DEV CLASS"/PROJECT6
kubectl delete -k .

Step 2: Confirm the load balancer is actually gone before proceeding

bash
aws elbv2 describe-load-balancers --region us-east-1

Wait until this returns an empty list (can take 1-2 minutes). Don't skip this check.

Step 3: Now destroy the Terraform infrastructure (VPC, EKS cluster, node group — the expensive stuff)

bash
cd terraform
terraform destroy



The Complete 4-Step Startup Routine

    Start Minikube:
    Bash

    minikube start -p project7

    Restart the backend deployment (to fix the database connection):
    Bash

    kubectl rollout restart deployment/backend-deployment -n quiz-namespace

    Wait for the backend rollout to finish:
    Bash

    kubectl rollout status deployment/backend-deployment -n quiz-namespace

    Launch the frontend service:
    Bash

    minikube service frontend-service -n quiz-namespace -p project7